":"If the council is forced to borrow at elevated PWLB rates (now 5.28%-6.29% depending on term), the under-borrowed position becomes expensive; the strategy was not hedged against rate rises.","iso":"2026-09-08","paper_title":"ANNUAL REPORT ON THE TREASURY MANAGEMENT SERVICE AND ACTUAL PRUDENTIAL INDICATORS 2025/26","paper_url":"https://tamworth.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45202/45202.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"General Fund capital receipts fell 67% from \u00a310.582m to \u00a33.472m, reducing financial flexibility","lead":"Limits flexibility for future capital spending and increases reliance on external borrowing; reduces buffer for unexpected capital needs.","iso":"2026-09-08","paper_title":"ANNUAL REPORT ON THE TREASURY MANAGEMENT SERVICE AND ACTUAL PRUDENTIAL INDICATORS 2025/26","paper_url":"https://tamworth.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45202/45202.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"\u00a323.6m of planned capital expenditure re-profiled to 2026/27; actual spend 32% below budget","lead":"May indicate delays in capital project delivery or changes to spending priorities; affects medium-term capital financing requirements and borrowing plans.","iso":"2026-09-08","paper_title":"ANNUAL REPORT ON THE TREASURY MANAGEMENT SERVICE AND ACTUAL PRUDENTIAL INDICATORS 2025/26","paper_url":"https://tamworth.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45202/45202.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-08","committee":"Council","paper_title":"ANNUAL REPORT ON THE TREASURY MANAGEMENT SERVICE AND ACTUAL PRUDENTIAL INDICATORS 2025/26","paper_url":"https://tamworth.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45202/45202.pdf","headline":"Market interest rate expectations have reversed; council's under-borrowed strategy faces rising rate risk","detail":"The council adopted an under-borrowed position for 2025/26, using cash reserves rather than borrowing because investment returns were expected to exceed borrowing costs. However, market expectations have since shifted sharply. As of early April 2026, the market expects Bank Rate to rise from 3.75% to 4-4.25%, contrary to forecasts of reductions made when the strategy was set. The Middle East conflict is expected to push inflation above 4.5%, potentially requiring higher rates. This reversal threatens the fundamental assumption underpinning the under-borrowed strategy.","category":"borrowing","matters":"If the council is forced to borrow at elevated PWLB rates (now 5.28%-6.29% depending on term), the under-borrowed position becomes expensive; the strategy was not hedged against rate rises.","figures":"Current market expects rates to rise to 4-4.25% (from 3.75%); PWLB 5-year rate now 5.28%, 10-year 5.72%; CPI forecast to exceed 4.5%","quote":"At the start of April 2026, the market expected Bank Rate to increase over the coming months to 4% or 4.25%, from 3.75%... More recently, the Middle East conflict is likely to see inflation spike higher from late spring 2026 through to early 2027.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-08","committee":"Council","paper_title":"ANNUAL REPORT ON THE TREASURY MANAGEMENT SERVICE AND ACTUAL PRUDENTIAL INDICATORS 2025/26","paper_url":"https://tamworth.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45202/45202.pdf","headline":"General Fund capital receipts fell 67% from \u00a310.582m to \u00a33.472m, reducing financial flexibility","detail":"Usable capital receipts available to the General Fund dropped by \u00a37.110m, a two-thirds reduction. The document notes these receipts were initially allocated to property fund investments. This substantial depletion significantly constrains the council's ability to fund capital projects or respond to one-off needs without external borrowing.","category":"reserves","matters":"Limits flexibility for future capital spending and increases reliance on external borrowing; reduces buffer for unexpected capital needs.","figures":"31st March 2025: \u00a310.582m; 31st March 2026: \u00a33.472m; decline of \u00a37.110m","quote":"Usable Capital Receipts 10.582 3.472","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-08","committee":"Council","paper_title":"ANNUAL REPORT ON THE TREASURY MANAGEMENT SERVICE AND ACTUAL PRUDENTIAL INDICATORS 2025/26","paper_url":"https://tamworth.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45202/45202.pdf","headline":"\u00a323.6m of planned capital expenditure re-profiled to 2026/27; actual spend 32% below budget","detail":"Budgeted capital expenditure of \u00a325.288m was significantly underspent at \u00a317.201m actual. The council notes that \u00a323.6m of capital scheme spend (including re-profiling from prior years) has been shifted into 2026/27. This timing shift boosted 2025/26 investment balances but raises questions about capital programme delivery and execution.","category":"capital","matters":"May indicate delays in capital project delivery or changes to spending priorities; affects medium-term capital financing requirements and borrowing plans.","figures":"Budgeted \u00a325.288m; actual \u00a317.201m; \u00a323.6m re-profiled to next year","quote":"It should be noted that \u00a323.6m of Capital scheme spend has been re-profiled into 2026/27 (also including re-profiling from previous years)","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-08","committee":"Council","paper_title":"ANNUAL REPORT ON THE TREASURY MANAGEMENT SERVICE AND ACTUAL PRUDENTIAL INDICATORS 2025/26","paper_url":"https://tamworth.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45202/45202.pdf","headline":"Hermes property fund investment became ineligible following fund merger in August 2025","detail":"The council had invested \u00a34.057m with Hermes Federated Property Unit Trust as part of a \u00a312m Commercial Investment Strategy expecting 4-5% returns over 5-10 years. In August 2025, Hermes merged with L&G's Managed Property Fund, which restricts eligibility to UK Pensions only. The council's investment had to be liquidated and reinvested in money market funds and fixed-term investments, disrupting the intended strategy. The remaining property fund portfolio has declined \u00a378k in 2025/26 alone and shows cumulative losses of \u00a31.12m against the initial investment, though \u00a32.317m in dividends have been received to date.","category":"investment","matters":"Demonstrates external constraints on council commercial strategy and concentration risk; also shows underperformance of a strategic long-term investment meant to improve returns.","figures":"\u00a34.057m forced liquidation; remaining property funds valued at \u00a36.786m; cumulative loss \u00a31.12m since inception; dividends \u00a32.317m cumulative","quote":"In August 2025, The Hermes Federated Property Unit Trust merged with L&G's Managed Property Fund and Tamworth Borough Council were not eligible to remain Unit Holders in the new merged fund as this is limited to UK Pensions only.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-08-20","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"ANNUAL REPORT ON THE TREASURY MANAGEMENT SERVICE AND ACTUAL PRUDENTIAL INDICATORS 2025/26","paper_url":"https://tamworth.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45132/45132.pdf","headline":"No new borrowing undertaken, with council citing elevated long-term rates as constraint","detail":"The council undertook no external borrowing in 2025/26 despite a Capital Financing Requirement of \u00a374m and external debt of \u00a363m. The council maintained an under-borrowed position, using cash reserves instead. The reason given is the elevated cost of long-term borrowing (PWLB rates peaked above 5%), which the council judged unaffordable.","category":"borrowing","matters":"The decision not to borrow despite capital needs signals that the council views borrowing as unaffordable at current rates and is instead consuming reserves\u2014a short-term strategy that cannot continue indefinitely as reserves deplete.","figures":"\u00a30m new borrowing; external debt \u00a363.060m (unchanged); CFR \u00a374.027m; average external investments \u00a349.1m; 1-year PWLB rate was 5.04% at 31 March 2026","quote":"Due to the elevated cost of borrowing long-term, no borrowing was undertaken during the year.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-08"},"name":"Tamworth","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-17","since_iso":"2026-08-18","distress":false,"headline":"Market interest rate expectations have reversed; council's under-borrowed strategy faces rising rate risk","lead":"If the council is forced to borrow at elevated PWLB rates (now 5.28%-6.29% depending on term), the under-borrowed position becomes expensive; the strategy was not hedged against rate rises.","also":["8 other findings in this window","9 papers caught"],"watch":"Cabinet on 2026-09-17","n_findings":9,"n_documents":9,"top_paper_title":"ANNUAL REPORT ON THE TREASURY MANAGEMENT SERVICE AND ACTUAL PRUDENTIAL INDICATORS 2025/26","top_paper_url":"https://tamworth.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45202/45202.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":67,"news_drafts_total":3}}